1 of 15. Meena received a ₹2,000 kurta and reports it damaged 45 days after delivery. How long after delivery can a customer still ask for a refund, and what happens after that?
  A) Refund possible up to 30 days after delivery; after that she gets nothing  B) Refund possible up to 15 days after delivery; after that she gets nothing  C) Refund possible up to 30 days, and after that you decide case by case (damaged items may still get a refund)
  Your answer (a letter, or your own words) > 
  > Damaged goods get a full refund up to 90 days after delivery, and after that nothing. So Meena at 45 days still gets her full ₹2,000 back, and the shipping fee too.

2 of 15. Anita returns a ₹1,000 unused item because she changed her mind, within the allowed days. Do you keep any part of the money as a handling charge?
  A) No charge: she gets the full item price back  B) You keep 10% (₹100) as a handling fee  C) You keep a fixed ₹150 regardless of price
  Your answer (a letter, or your own words) > 
  > A) No charge: she gets the full item price back, minus the discount code if she used one. I only hold back the shipping fee, and the shipping fee is refunded only if the goods were damaged.
  (Your answer also settles 2 of the coming questions; I'll skip them.)

4 of 15. Priya's refund of ₹1,200 is approved. Does the money go back to her bank card or as shop credit to spend with you?
  A) Damaged: back to her card. Changed mind: shop credit only  B) Always back to her card  C) Always shop credit, unless the customer insists and you approve
  Your answer (a letter, or your own words) > 
  > The money goes back the way it was paid: card orders go back to the card, and store credit orders go back as store credit. So none of these options is exactly right.

5 of 15. Karan says his refund reason is 'wrong colour sent', 'arrived very late', or just leaves the reason blank. How should each be treated?
  A) Shop's mistake (wrong item, etc.): treated like damaged, full refund to card; unclear or blank: treated like changed mind  B) Everything other than 'damaged' is treated like changed mind  C) Any blank or unclear reason is held for you to decide personally
  Your answer (a letter, or your own words) > 
  > Wrong colour sent or arrived very late: I'd treat it like damaged, so full refund including shipping, up to 90 days. A blank reason is treated like a normal change of mind, so the 14 and 30 day rules apply and shipping is not refunded.

7 of 15. A customer asks for a refund on an order that was already refunded once, or one that was cancelled, or one that has not been delivered yet. What happens?
  A) Already refunded: nothing more is paid. Not yet delivered: full refund to the card  B) Already refunded: nothing. Not yet delivered: refund only after the parcel comes back to you  C) Any such request goes to you for a decision, with no automatic payment
  Your answer (a letter, or your own words) > 
  > Not exactly any of those. If an order was already refunded, nothing more is paid. I haven't set a rule for cancelled or not-yet-delivered orders, so nothing should be paid automatically on those.

8 of 15. A customer spends ₹1,250 on an order. How many loyalty points does she earn?
  A) 1 point for every full ₹100, so 12 points (the leftover ₹50 earns nothing)  B) 1 point for every ₹10, so 125 points  C) 1 point for every ₹100 but rounded to the nearest, so 13 points
  Your answer (a letter, or your own words) > 
  > A) 1 point for every full ₹100 of items paid, after the discount code. So ₹1,250 gives 12 points, but only if the items come to ₹500 or more, and not for store credit orders.
  (Your answer also settles 1 of the coming question; I'll skip it.)

10 of 15. Deepa earns points on a ₹1,000 order, then returns it for a refund. Another customer pays ₹1,000 half with store credit. What happens to the points?
  A) Refunded or cancelled orders earn 0 points, and the part paid with store credit earns none  B) Points stay with the customer even after a refund  C) Points are given first, then taken back when the refund is made, and store credit earns points like cash
  Your answer (a letter, or your own words) > 
